[?2004hroot@1988364556ea:/app# cat >> README.md <<'EOF'
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Resume and crash recovery
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The checkpoint's `last_id` is **inclusive**: it is the last committed live
[?2004l[?2004h> source document. The source API's start ID is also inclusive, so resume reads
[?2004l[?2004h> from `last_id + 1` (IDs need not be contiguous).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Each commit flushes and fsyncs all shard bytes, including shards closed during
[?2004l[?2004h> rotation, then syncs the index directory. The checkpoint records the byte length
[?2004l[?2004h> of every committed shard in `shards`. It is written to a temporary file, fsynced,
[?2004l[?2004h> atomically renamed over the previous checkpoint, and its directory is synced.
[?2004l[?2004h> On restart, recovery truncates uncommitted tails and removes uncommitted shards
[?2004l[?2004h> before reading more source documents. Recovery is idempotent, including after a
[?2004l[?2004h> kill during recovery. Missing committed bytes are reported as an error rather
[?2004l[?2004h> than silently skipping source documents. Old checkpoints without byte offsets
[?2004l[?2004h> cannot prove which rows survived and are rebuilt once.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP request a stop after the current document and commit;
[?2004l[?2004h> the worker exits 75 when incomplete. SIGKILL can lose uncommitted work only: the
[?2004l[?2004h> next run restores the last durable checkpoint. Supervisor budgets, cooldowns,
[?2004l[?2004h> and row transformations are unchanged. Run `./jobs reindex` again after each
[?2004l[?2004h> budget stop until `./jobs status` reports `complete: True`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The build assumes the source corpus remains stable over the reindex. To build a
[?2004l[?2004h> new snapshot after source changes, use `./jobs clean` and start a fresh reindex.
